WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.240
Light sets the clock and the pupil.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.940
Can it also change mood and learning, apart from sleep?

3
00:00:09.933 --> 00:00:13.933
LeGates and colleagues tested this in mice, with a T7 cycle.

4
00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:19.333
That is three and a half hours of light, then three and a half hours of dark, again

5
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:20.333
and again.

6
00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:24.517
T7 mice showed depression-like behaviour.

7
00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:28.127
Long-term potentiation in hippocampal slices was impaired.

8
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.830
Mice lacking ipRGCs did not show these changes.

9
00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:35.273
Two controls matter.

10
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:39.213
Earlier work found total sleep preserved under T7.

11
00:00:40.283 --> 00:00:44.243
And the clock stayed rhythmic, although its circadian period lengthened.

12
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:48.787
As with any ablation, this implicates the cells, not the pigment.

13
00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:51.607
And a mouse assay is not a diagnosis.

14
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.040
Predict. Do the mood and learning effects run through the same output, or through

15
00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:59.040
different ones?

16
00:01:04.583 --> 00:01:06.963
Fernandez and colleagues found two routes.

17
00:01:08.003 --> 00:01:12.143
Cells projecting mainly to the SCN were sufficient for the learning deficits.

18
00:01:13.233 --> 00:01:17.813
A separate path, to the perihabenular region next to the habenula, carried the mood

19
00:01:17.813 --> 00:01:18.813
effects.

20
00:01:19.167 --> 00:01:23.667
In the tested paradigms, silencing this region prevented the mood-like change.

21
00:01:24.647 --> 00:01:26.567
Activating it was enough to produce one.

22
00:01:27.517 --> 00:01:31.017
Whether a matching circuit exists in humans was not tested.

23
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.440
A smaller story asks whether these cells help with seeing patterns.

24
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.500
Some of them send axons to the thalamic visual relay.

25
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:44.773
Mice without rod and cone signalling could tell a striped grating from grey.

26
00:01:45.433 --> 00:01:46.933
But only very coarse stripes.

27
00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:52.233
Mice lacking melanopsin showed deficits in contrast sensitivity.

28
00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:54.913
Still, this is a secondary role.

29
00:01:55.867 --> 00:01:56.967
What about people?

30
00:01:58.047 --> 00:02:02.027
Zaidi and colleagues studied two blind adults without functional rods or cones.

31
00:02:03.117 --> 00:02:09.157
In one woman, the pupil was most sensitive near 480 nanometres, after correcting for

32
00:02:09.157 --> 00:02:10.157
the lens.

33
00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.980
She also detected a light above chance in a forced-choice test.

34
00:02:14.983 --> 00:02:19.483
In one man, light of 460 nanometres suppressed melatonin.

35
00:02:20.567 --> 00:02:22.727
Two results, from two different people.

36
00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:26.867
Together they are an existence proof, not a population result.

37
00:02:27.917 --> 00:02:30.117
Evening screens are a separate question.

38
00:02:31.037 --> 00:02:35.477
In one study, people read a bright device for about four hours, on five evenings.

39
00:02:36.567 --> 00:02:41.707
Melatonin was lower than in a dim-light test, and its evening rise came later than

40
00:02:41.707 --> 00:02:42.727
after a printed book.

41
00:02:43.527 --> 00:02:46.007
But the study did not separate spectrum from brightness.

42
00:02:46.927 --> 00:02:48.687
The link to these cells is an inference.

43
00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.600
The same picture helps across these mouse results.

44
00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:57.240
Each cell pools two light signals, and different cells reach different targets.

45
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.640
It does not explain the image-forming subtypes, and it cannot test necessity in

46
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:02.900
people.
